.TH TIME 1
.SH NAME
time \- time a command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B time
command
[
argument ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
given command is executed; after it is complete,
.I time
prints the elapsed time during the command, the time
spent in the system, and the time spent in execution
of the command.
Times are reported in hours, minutes, seconds and tenths of a second, with leading
zeros suppressed.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page said `Times are reported in seconds', which was never quite what the program
printed.
.PP
The times are printed on the diagnostic output stream.
.PP
The exit status is the command's own.
.B Note:
v7's page did not say so.
.SH "BESM-6 NOTES"
.SS A tick is a 250th of a second, not a 60th
.IR Times (2)
counts in 1/HZ seconds and
.B HZ is 250 here,
where the PDP-11's line clock ran at 60.  Both places v7's source built that number in \-
the conversion of elapsed seconds to ticks, and the leading entry of the radix table that
decodes ticks back into tenths \- are derived from
.B CLOCKS_PER_SEC
now.  The printed layout is unchanged.
.SS The separators are no longer NUL bytes
v7's
.I printt
held \e0 in its separator tables to mean `nothing between these two digits', and printed
it anyway.  On a terminal that is invisible, which is the only place v7 looked; in a file
it is a NUL, which is what
.B "time cmd 2>log"
produces.  Fixed: the separator is emitted only when there is one.
.SS The paragraph about MOS and core is gone
It said that execution time depends on which kind of memory the program lands in, and that
user time in MOS is often half what it is in core.  That is PDP-11/45 hardware, and this
machine has none of it.
.SH BUGS
Elapsed time is accurate to the second, being the difference of two
.IR time (2)
calls, while the CPU times are measured to the 250th.
Thus the sum of the CPU times can be up to a second larger
than the elapsed time.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
times(2), time(2)
